Manuscript: Extended Song Profiling & Backup System
I. Data Architecture: The "Shadow Profile"
Standard Android MediaStore only provides basic info (Title, Artist). You must build a custom Local Database Schema to store the "Extra Info."

1. The Song Profile Table (songs_metadata)
Every time a song is scanned, create an entry here. Do not rely on the file's ID3 tags for dynamic data like play counts.

file_path (Primary Key): The unique identifier for the song.

play_count (Integer): Increments only when a song reaches the 90% completion mark.

last_played_timestamp: The exact date/time the song was last opened.

date_added_to_app: Different from the file's "date modified."

is_favorite (Boolean): A quick toggle for the "Favorites" playlist.

bookmark_position (Long): Stores the exact millisecond where the user left off (crucial for podcasts/long sets).

2. The Playlist Mapping Table (playlist_entries)
Instead of storing playlists as separate files (like .m3u), store them as a many-to-many relationship table.

playlist_id | song_file_path | entry_order

This allows a single song to belong to 100 playlists without duplicating the audio file.

II. Implementation of the Backup Engine
The goal is to create a single .mus (or .zip) file that can restore the entire app state on a brand-new phone.

1. The Backup Package Structure
When the user taps "Backup Now," the app should generate a ZIP container containing:

database_export.db: A copy of your SQLite/Room database file.

preferences.json: A serialized version of all SharedPreferences (Theme, Tab order, Equalizer settings).

metadata.json: A file containing the Backup Date, App Version, and a checksum for security.

2. The "Path Re-mapping" Logic (Critical)
The biggest challenge is moving backups between phones where the Music folder path might change (e.g., from an SD card to internal storage).

The Solution: Store relative paths. Instead of /storage/emulated/0/Music/Song.mp3, store [ROOT]/Music/Song.mp3.

On Restore: The app should ask the user to "Locate your Music Folder." Once identified, the app automatically swaps [ROOT] with the new device's path.

III. Logic for Stats & Attributes

Feature	Logic Trigger	Storage Method
Play Count	Triggered when CurrentPosition > (Duration * 0.9).	UPDATE songs_metadata SET play_count = play_count + 1 WHERE path = current_path
Last Played	Triggered on onStartCommand of the Media Player.	Store System.currentTimeMillis() in the DB.
A-B Repeat	User marks Start and End timestamps.	Store in a temporary_playback_state table.


IV. Instructions for Your Developer Agent
"Implement a Persistent Data System using Room Database.

Extended Profiles: Create a table that tracks play_counts, last_played, and is_favorite for every file path.

Play Count Logic: Add an OnCompletionListener to the player that increments the count only if the song played for more than 30 seconds or 90% of its length.

Backup System: Build a service that exports the Room .db file and SharedPreferences into a single ZIP archive.

Smart Restore: When importing a backup, implement a Path Resolver that detects if the file paths have changed (e.g., due to a new device) and offers a 'Bulk Update Path' tool to link the old metadata to the new file locations."